WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
File paths cross trust boundaries in many ordinary workflows.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:15.960
A command-line argument, configuration value, environment variable, package setting, administrator-provided path, or runtime-generated path can all guide file operations.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.940
Each source needs a trust decision based on who controls it and what the program will do with it.

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:25.380
User-supplied paths should be validated against the intended purpose.

5
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:35.080
A path used for input is different from a path used for output, cleanup, replacement, import, export, or logging.

6
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:40.300
The validation rule should match the operation, not just the fact that the value is a path.

7
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.180
Environment-derived paths are not automatically trusted.

8
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.320
Environment variables can be absent, unexpected, inherited from a launcher, or changed by deployment tooling.

9
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.340
Service working directories can also differ between development and production.

10
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.400
Code should make its directory assumptions visible.

11
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:08.680
Shared directories require stricter assumptions because other users or processes may be able to create, remove, or replace entries depending on permissions.

12
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:17.660
Temporary directories, spool directories, upload directories, and package-managed paths all deserve explicit ownership and permission review.

13
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.540
Configuration paths should match documented purpose.

14
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:31.400
If a setting names a trusted root, output directory, key location, log directory, or cache path, the program should validate it as that role.

15
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:39.820
Directory trust depends on ownership, permissions, who can modify entries, and whether the program runs with broader access than the path source.
